PRE-TABLE CHECK -- #496 gdromcondlies
=====================================
WRITTEN BEFORE TABLE 1 RUNS -- and the ORDER still holds, because no table has run.

*** BUT THE MTIME PROOF THIS FILE CLAIMED AS ITS OWN WARRANT NO LONGER EXISTS, AND THAT
IS A DEFECT IN THE ARTIFACT RATHER THAN IN THE ORDER. ***  The header used to read
"its mtime is the proof of that order".  The file was then edited twice on the same day
-- 9,184 bytes at 16:03, 13,812 at 16:21 -- and first committed at 16:39, so git holds no
earlier blob and the mtime proves only the last edit.  Found by the opus5 seat.

**THE FIX, and it matters because this artifact is now MANDATORY for every kill-table
round: corrections go in a sibling `PRETABLE_ADDENDA.out`, leaving the block itself
immutable.**  An artifact whose warrant is its own timestamp may not be edited in place.
The pass-2 amendment below predates that ruling and is left where it is rather than moved,
since moving it now would be a third edit to the same file.

The order discriminator is the same one the oracle-correction rule uses.  Mandatory since 2026-08-28 for any
round shipping or extending a kill-table-backed detector.

THE ROUND:  two live BADVAL host kills in src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c.
  SITE A  GDROM_UNKNOWN_0x88 write, any value != 0x0b
  SITE B  GDROM_COND write, any value not in {0xa0, 0xef}
FIX SHAPE: latched decline at both (the house idiom, N=1 per device instance, "(once)"),
plus -- at site B only -- RELOCATING `d->cond = idata;` from above the dispatch into the
two accepted arms.

WITNESS: rung 3, regress/artifacts/gdromcondlies/witness_20260830/, 15/15 on the committed
build before any edit.  Both kills reproduce on an unmodified -E dreamcast, one guest
mov.w each.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1.  SIBLING PARITY
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(i) SITE A's write arm vs SITE A's OWN READ ARM.  ASYMMETRY, AND IT IS REAL.
    The read arm seven lines above (`fatal("Read from GDROM_UNKNOWN_0x88?")`, exit
    commented out) is UNLATCHED and floods -- measured 3 accesses / 3 diagnostics in
    extras_run1.out.  This round ships a `case` whose write arm announces once and whose
    read arm one line away announces without bound.
    DISPOSITION: FILED, not taken.  The round-scope rule's third arm fails -- a flood is
    not the BADVAL class -- and taking :1089 alone would split the flood FAMILY, which is
    already carried as `floodclass`.  The two Agent seats DISAGREED here (opus5: take it;
    fable5: file it) and the disagreement is settled against the rule's own text, not by
    vote.  The cost is recorded in the round block, which is where a scope cost belongs.
    OWED SENTENCE: yes, in the round block.  Not a row.

(ii) SITE B's decline vs handle_command()'s decline.  ASYMMETRY, AND THE TWO IN-FILE
    PRECEDENTS CONTRADICT EACH OTHER.
      #490's default arm FIRES a completion event on decline -- its comment says an early
        return "leaves a guest waiting for a completion that never comes".
      The DMA arm REFUSES one -- its comment says "a completion on a DECLINED transfer
        would be a FALSE SUCCESS".
    They disagree because the WAIT STATES differ.  At GDROM_COND the guest has sent no
    packet and is polling BUSY, so an event would announce a command that never began.
    RULING: no event on the decline, with BOTH citations in the comment.
    OWED ROW: yes -- R7 below.  This is a semantic would-consequence claim and the
    verb-list form of CLAIM->ROW walks straight past it.

(iii) SITE A's decline message vs SITE B's.  #461's measured rule in this same file is
    that the write line is "the only one carrying idata, which is the value needed to
    model the register later".  BOTH new messages print the value.  No asymmetry.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.  CLAIM -> ROW   (every added comment sentence asserting an OBSERVABLE CONSEQUENCE)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The trigger is SEMANTIC, not the five-verb grep: does this sentence assert something a
row could observe?  A "would <consequence>" clause qualifies.

 C1  "an unmodelled value is declined, not fatal"        (site A)   -> R1
 C2  "an unmodelled value is declined"                   (site B)   -> R2
 C3  "ignored.  (once)" / "(once)"                       (both)     -> R3, R4
 C4  "d->cond left unchanged"                            (site B)   -> R5  *** keystone
 C5  "Accepted values keep their old semantics byte for
      byte, store included and in the same order"        (site B)   -> R6, R8, R9
 C6  "NO COMPLETION EVENT ON THE DECLINE ... an event
      would announce a command that never began"         (site B)   -> R7
 C7  "0x0b is accepted and is a no-op, touching no
      device state"                                      (site A)   -> R10
 C8  the two latches are SEPARATE bits                   (both)     -> R11

NO CLAIM IS LEFT WITHOUT A ROW.  If the row list is later rewritten from pass-2 findings,
it is DIFFED AGAINST THIS LIST, not merely extended with the new rows -- #490 lost two
prescribed rows exactly that way, three hours after its own PRETABLE named them.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3.  GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SITE A   guard reads: idata.            body reads/writes: d->warned only.
SITE B   guard reads: idata.            body reads/writes: d->cond, d->stat, d->busy,
                                        d->cmd_count, d->warned, and fires SYSASIC.

The guard operand (idata) is a call parameter with no second writer, so the classic
"swap the guard's operand for a body field" form has no candidate here.

*** THE CHECK THEREFORE DEGENERATES TO THE SHARED-BITFIELD QUESTION, which is the form
#490 measured and which the guard/body grep CANNOT see: who else writes d->warned, and
can they set MY bit? ***
    d->warned |= ...   -- *** THESE TWO LINES WERE WRONG.  CORRECTED IN PLACE 2026-08-30,
    because a reader who greps the wrong number must meet its correction HERE and not 110
    lines away, which is where the amendment sat. ***

        grep -c 'd->warned |=' <file>   ->  12 RAW, ONE of them inside a comment
        LIVE writers                    ->  11   (9 before this round)
        inside a DEVICE_ACCESS          ->   8   (6 before this round)

    The original said "8 before, 10 after" -- #490's numbers with its own two additions
    never folded in -- and "SIX of the eight ... set by ORDINARY GUEST REGISTER READS".
    Not all are reads: NOTWAIT, DMANODATA and DMAARM are write-triggered, LEN is
    width-triggered on both directions, and both new bits are write-triggered.  The hazard
    is UNCHANGED and if anything broader -- guest traffic of either direction can pre-set
    a colliding bit.
    So a bit collision is silenced by benign traffic and looks like a working latch.
OWED ROW: R11 -- both sites decline in ONE session and BOTH announce.  #490's `crossbit`
is the precedent; its `M3shared` escape is this file's recorded cost of skipping it.

STRUCTURAL GUARD, and it is why no row is owed for the enum itself: the disjointness
assert is a TYPEDEF'd negative-array-size check that does not extend itself.  Adding an
enumerator without extending the sum is a COMPILE ERROR, not a silent pass.  Verified by
construction this round -- the tree built, therefore the extension is correct.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4.  CHANNEL  -- per row: the mutant it kills, and the observable that DIFFERS under it
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*** THE ONE CHANNEL THIS ROUND MAY NOT USE: SURVIVAL. ***  Post-fix everything exits 0,
so a survival oracle at a refusal-effect site is vacuous BY CONSTRUCTION.  The witness's
own accept rows discriminate on `how == exit0` and therefore MUST NOT be carried into the
detector unchanged.  (fable5, pass 1, before any table ran.)

 row  channel observed                          kills
 ---  ----------------------------------------  --------------------------------------
 R1   site-A decline diagnostic PRESENT         M1  reinstate exit(1) at A        (b)
 R2   site-B decline diagnostic PRESENT         M2  reinstate exit(1) at B        (b)
 R3   site-A diagnostic count == 1 over 3       M3  latch dropped at A
 R4   site-B diagnostic count == 1 over 3       M4  latch dropped at B
 R5   COND READBACK after a DECLINED write      M5  d->cond = idata restored to
      *** ANCHORED: drive an ACCEPTED 0xef          the top of the else            (d)
      FIRST, then the declined write, then      M5b d->cond = 0 on decline
      require 0xef back.  A fresh device has
      cond == 0, so "expect 0" is satisfied
      by the fix AND by a zeroing mutant --
      the anchor is what separates them. ***
 R6   BUSY bit 0x08 SET after accepting 0xa0    M6  the 0xa0 arm emptied
 R7   NO ASIC event pending after a DECLINED    M7  an event fired on the decline
      COND write
 R8   COND readback == 0xa0 after accepting     M8  the accepted-path store dropped
 R9   ASIC event pending after accepting 0xef   M9  the 0xef arm emptied
 R10  no device state changes when A accepts    M10 site A grew an effect
      0x0b (stat/busy/cond unchanged)
 R11  BOTH sites announce in ONE session        M11 the two latch bits collapsed
                                                    into one

EVERY ROW'S NAMED CHANNEL CAN VARY UNDER ITS NAMED MUTANT.  Checked one by one; none is
a survival row; none is green-under-every-candidate.

CLAUSE (d) ENUMERATION -- "DEPENDS ON", walked up only to the enclosing function:
  predicates DOMINATING the edited lines: `idata != 0xb` (A), `idata == 0xa0` and
  `idata == 0xef` (B), and the two `!(d->warned & ...)` latch guards.
  operand-swap candidates drawn ONLY from body-read fields: d->cond, d->busy,
  d->cmd_count, d->warned.
  -> M5 (the store relocation nulled) is the canonical member and is the round's most
  load-bearing mutant.  BOTH Agent seats named it INDEPENDENTLY on pass 1, and both said
  every row in the brief's then-current plan stayed green under it.  R5 exists because
  of that.

CLAUSE (f) ANCHOR: R5 is the anchored row.  R6/R8/R9 are anchored by side effect
(a bit the device itself sets; an event the device itself fires).

CLAUSE (e) CONTRACT: to be written into the probe header before the table runs.  It
claims the eleven mutants above and DISCLAIMS everything else -- notably any edit to the
GDROM_DATA read path, which this detector does not observe.

PRETABLE_DONE

===============================================================================
PASS-2 AMENDMENT -- written after the pass-2 panel, BEFORE table 1 still runs.
===============================================================================

*** THE ROW LIST GREW FROM 16 TO 20, AND PER #490 IT IS DIFFED AGAINST THE PRESCRIBED
ROWS ABOVE RATHER THAN MERELY EXTENDED. ***

#490 wrote a PRETABLE naming two mutants at 16:05, had a panel find a different defect at
18:45, rewrote the addition list to carry the panel's findings -- and BOTH earlier
prescribed rows silently vanished.  A measuring seat then built both and both escaped the
committed detector: two clause-(d) floor breaches the round's own artifact had predicted.

THE DIFF, explicitly:

    prescribed above and STILL PRESENT:  R1 R2 R3 R4 R5 R6 R7 R8 R9 R10 R11   (11 of 11)
    dropped:                             NONE
    added by pass 2:                     R1b  R5b  R5c  R7b                   (4)

    total 20 rows = 4 controls + 16 detector rows.

WHY EACH ADDITION EXISTS -- each closes a MEASURED-or-DERIVED escape through the 16-row
form, named by the codex seat on pass 2:

  R1b  Site A's oracle counted a PLAIN SUBSTRING, so `0x%02x` -> `0x%02d` in its format
       string escaped: guest byte 0x55 reports as "85" while every presence, latch and
       cross-bit row stays green.  A PRESENCE ORACLE CANNOT SEE A FIDELITY DEFECT.  R1 now
       captures the byte and R1b writes a SECOND, different value so a constant cannot
       masquerade as the datum.  (Site B already had this shape -- the asymmetry was the
       defect, and SIBLING PARITY should have caught it above.  It did not.)

  R5b  } R5 alone pins "cond == 0xef after this sequence", which the correct fix satisfies
  R5c  } and so does `d->cond = 0xef;` in the decline arm -- a mutant that fabricates
       } COND_DATA_AVAIL out of a FRESH decline and unlocks the GDROM_DATA read path.
       } *** ONE ANCHOR PROVES A VALUE, NOT AN IDENTITY. ***  "Unchanged" is a claim about
       } a FUNCTION of the prior value and one sample cannot distinguish a function from a
       } constant.  Three pre-states (fresh 0x00, accepted 0xa0, accepted 0xef) separate
       } the identity from any constant, any zeroing, any OR, and idata itself.

  R7b  R5* pin `cond` and R7 pins the event; a one-line busy/stat mutation in the decline
       arm passed BOTH.  R7b is a BEFORE/AFTER comparison rather than an absolute, because
       writing the fresh values as literals would mean reading them off the binary and
       pinning them -- observationally identical to fitting the test to the answer.
       Guarded so an apparatus failure cannot make it self-compare to green.

CORRECTED COUNTS -- the GUARD FIELD section above was wrong and this records the deriver:

    grep -c 'd->warned |=' src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c        ->  12 RAW
    ... of which ONE is inside a comment block (the `* d->warned |= 0x40` example)
    LIVE writers                                                    ->  11  (9 + these 2)
    inside DEVICE_ACCESS                                            ->   8

The block above said "8 sites before, 10 after" and "SIX of the eight inside
DEVICE_ACCESS".  Both wrong.  *** AND THE RAW GREP THAT PRODUCED 12 IS THE SAME
COMMENT-CONTAMINATION THIS PROJECT BUILT `exit_census.py` TO DEFEAT -- 40% of raw exit(
hits in src/devices/ are inside comments, and the same hazard applies to any grep over
this tree.  The count was corrected by a seat, then re-derived here by listing the sites
and reading the one that was a comment. ***

REFINEMENT, also from the seat, and it narrows a claim rather than overturning it: the six
pre-existing DEVICE_ACCESS writers are NOT all guest register READS -- GDROM_W_NOTWAIT,
GDROM_W_DMANODATA and GDROM_W_DMAARM are write-triggered.  The collision premise is
unaffected (benign guest traffic can still pre-set a colliding bit, which is what makes a
latch-bit collision silent), but the wording "set by ordinary guest register reads" was too
narrow and is corrected here rather than left to be reused.

TWO PRODUCT-CODE RECORDS CORRECTED IN THE SAME PASS (L4, wrong record):
  * "Nine bits today" above the disjointness assert -- #496 made it ELEVEN.  Now carries
    its deriver (`grep -c '_B,'` over the enum) so the next edit cannot silently stale it.
  * The decline comment said "the guest has sent no packet", which is too broad: nothing
    stops a bad COND write arriving after a 0xa0 or midway through a packet.  The warrant
    is that THIS WRITE began nothing, not that nothing is in flight.

PRETABLE_AMENDED_DONE
